  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1230 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nSaturday begins the long term period of with a bout of widespread \nwindy conditions associated with a shortwave trough clipping \nnortheastern NM. The attendant 100-110kt H3 jetmax rounding the \ntrough's southern periphery will overshoot the northern mountains \nand CO Rockies late Saturday, with 40-55kt H7 winds being the source \nof potentially high wind gusts impacting pockets of the central and \nadjacent highland areas. Cross-sectional analysis of numerical model \nruns show moderate to high potential for mountain wave activity lee \nof the Sangre de Cristo's and Sandia/Manzano Mts and/or vertical \nmixing to bring these stronger wind gusts to ground level Saturday \nafternoon. Confidence has risen just high enough to warrant the \nhoisting of a High Wind Watch over the Central Highland forecast \nzone focused on the Clines Corners area. Adjacent Wind Advisories \nfor gusty winds stretching along the northeast highlands from at \nleast Las Vegas to western portions of Guadalupe County will likely \nbe needed as well. Strong crosswinds/headwinds will be the main \nimpacts for travelers along area highways. Meanwhile, high \ntemperatures Saturday rise well above normal by 5F-15F ranging from \nthe upper 50s across western NM to 60s to low 70s in central and \neastern NM. \n\nThe trough axis quickly exits eastward Saturday night with a surface \ncold front backing southward through eastern NM into Sunday morning. \nStrong subsidence behind the front will aide a strong surface \npressure increase in the wake of the fropa, as much as 12mb/3hrs. \nThis will create a strong surface pressure gradient fueling gusty \nnortherly winds, as much as 35-50 mph along the TX border. Another \nslate of Wind Advisories may be necessary for this as we get closer. \nStrong CAA will also bring temperatures down sharply behind this \nfront for Sunday. Highs fall back 15F-25F across eastern NM, below \nnormal and ranging from the 40s to 50s. The temperature roller \ncoaster will be less intense further west into the Rio Grande Valley \nand toward the AZ border during this time. \n\nA warming trend takes hold Monday and Tuesday as breezy westerly to \nsouthwesterly winds arrive each afternoon. By Wednesday, there is a \ngood agreement among numerical models for a ~560dm H5 winter system \nto roll in from AZ bringing a mix of valley rain and mountain snow \nfavoring western, central, and northern NM.",
